title: Comrade Thomas
subtitle: The International's man in Berlin has money to offer.
new-page: true
tags: event
view-if: year = 1920 and month = 4 and crisis = 0 and ending_id = 0
max-visits: 1

= Comrade Thomas

The Communist International keeps a secret West European Secretariat in Berlin. Its head calls himself Comrade Thomas. He runs a publishing house, a network of couriers and a great deal of money, some of it in banknotes and some in jewels sold off from the treasures of the Tsars.

After Kapp, Thomas comes to the Zentrale with an offer. The party is short of everything: paper, printing presses, organisers' wages, money for the Reichstag election. Moscow would be glad to help, regularly, every month.

- @subsidy: Accept a regular subsidy.
- @once: Take money for the election, and nothing more.
- @refuse: Refuse. A German party must live on German workers' dues.

@subsidy
subtitle: One more fund every month. Moscow's money comes with Moscow's opinions.
on-arrival: {!
Q.moscow_subsidy += 1;
Q.resources += 1;
Q.independence -= 10;
Q.comintern_rep = 'Comrade Thomas';
Q.offensive_satisfaction += 3;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction -= 3;
!}

The money arrives on the first of every month, through a bank in Amsterdam. The party hires full-time secretaries in every district. They are paid by Moscow, and they know it.

@once
subtitle: 3 funds now. A smaller debt.
on-arrival: {!
Q.resources += 3;
Q.independence -= 3;
!}

Thomas hands over a sealed envelope and a receipt to sign. The election posters are printed the next week.

@refuse
subtitle: The Spartacist tradition. The party stays poor, and its own.
on-arrival: {!
Q.independence += 8;
Q.luxemburgist_satisfaction += 5;
Q.offensive_satisfaction -= 4;
Q.lean_luxemburgist += 1;
!}

Thomas takes his envelope away again. He will report to Zinoviev that the German Zentrale has delusions of independence.
